#There is another data stucture/variable type called a dictionary 
#A dict is a collection of key-value pairs - we assign a value to a key (something associated with something else)
#Similar to a list but instead of using an index to access the items in the collection we use a key to access the value/item associated with that key
#This is useful when we want to store data that has a relationship between the key and the value/item associated with that key
#It is more powerful than a list as we can use any type of data as a key (string, number, boolean, etc.) - we can also use any type of data as a value/item associated with that key (string, number, boolean, list, dict, etc.)


#CREATING A DICTIONARY
#Dict use curly braces {} to create a collection of key-value pairs - we use a colon : to separate the key from the value/item associated with that key - we use a comma , to separate each key-value pair in the collection
#We can write it as horizontal list or as a vertical list - vertical is easier to read and understand 
students = {
    "Herman":"Gryffindor",
    "Peter":"Gryffindor",
    "Paul":"Ravenclaw",
    "Mary":"Ravenclaw"
} 

print(students) #This will print the entire dictionary - all key-value pairs in the collection


#ACCESSING ITEMS IN A DICTIONARY
#We need to index into the dictionary to access the value/item associated with a key
#We can access the value/item associated with a key in a dictionary by using the key in square brackets []
print(students["Herman"]) #This will print "Gryffindor" - the key we use is "herman" to access the value/item associated with it in the dict  


#We can also use a for loop to iterate over the items in a dictionary
#When we use a for loop across a dictionary it only iterates over the keys and not the values/items (key=Herman and value = Gryffindor) 
#Remember we created the variable student in the for loop as temporary variable that takes on the value of each key in the dictionary as the loop iterates over it
for student in students:
    print(student)

for student in students:
    print(student, students[student]) 
    #This will print the key and the value/item associated with that key in the dict - we use the key to access the value/item associated with that key in the dict


#CREATING MORE VALUES/ITEMS IN A DICTIONARY
#We want to associate multiple things/items with a student 
#We create a list of dictionaries
#Each dictionary represents an individual student and contains key-value pairs for the student's name:item, house:item, and patronus:item - we can add more key-value pairs to each dictionary as needed 
#The square brackets [] are used to create a list - in this case of dictionaries which is each enclosed in curly braces {} and contains key-value pairs
students = [
    {"name": "Hermione", "house": "Gryffindor", "patronus": "Lion"},
    {"name": "Peter", "house": "Gryffindor", "patronus": "Stag"},
    {"name": "Paul", "house": "Ravenclaw", "patronus": "Eagle"},
    {"name": "Mary", "house": "Ravenclaw", "patronus": "none"}    
]
#We then close the list with the square brackets - we can now access each dictionary in the list by using the index of the list and then access the key-value pairs in the dictionary by using the key in square brackets []    

#We can use a for loop to iterate over the list of dictionaries and access the key-value pairs in each dictionary
for student in students:
    print(student["name"]) 

#We add multiple arguments to the print function to print more key-value pairs in each dictionary
for student in students:
    print(student["name"], student["house"], student["patronus"])

